session:pr812Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #780 was a follow-up from #591's original bug report, which explicitly listed 'OWL-RL-generated rules (vs. hand-written custom rules) interacting with deletion' as untested. #590 and #774's regression tests for IncrementalReasoner::apply_deletions all used hand-written datalog::Rule values mirroring a transitive-closure rule shape by hand; none used a rule actually compiled from an OWL ontology via owl2rl2datalog::owl2datalog, which can produce a materially different rule shape (e.g. from owl:TransitiveProperty, owl:equivalentClass, property chains). This is fundamentally a verification task that could have gone either way -- confirmed correct, or uncovered a real bug -- so the test design had to be trustworthy enough to actually tell the difference, not just pass trivially." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:812 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr812Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr812 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr812Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Added two regression tests exercising owl:TransitiveProperty and owl:equivalentClass, both compiled through the real owl2datalog pipeline (not hand-written), comparing against a from-scratch rebuild oracle -- the same pattern #590/#774 used -- with IncrementalReasoner::fallback_count() asserted unchanged so the test actually exercises the incremental BF path rather than the trivially-correct-by-construction full_rematerialise fallback (which would pass even if the incremental path were broken). fallback_count was previously a #[cfg(test)]-gated field, invisible to a test in a *different* crate (owl2rl2datalog depends on datalog, so datalog's own #[cfg(test)] only applies when datalog itself is compiled with --cfg test, not as a dependency) -- exposed as a public fallback_count() method instead of weakening the gate on the field itself. Both interactions came out correct: no production bug, durable coverage added for a previously-untested rule-compilation path." ;
    agp:alternative "The first version of both tests used isolated single-edge/unrelated-class padding facts to try to keep the deletion ratio under the 25% FALLBACK_THRESHOLD guard, following the same padding idea #590/#774's own hand-written-rule tests use. This was wrong for OWL-RL-compiled rules specifically: owl:TransitiveProperty's rule only derives a new fact from a *chain* of two edges, so an isolated single-edge padding fact derives nothing and contributes zero to the denominator; owl:equivalentClass's compiled rules are ground-class-specific (matching only class A or B, not a generic predicate), so a padding fact about an unrelated class never fires either rule either. Both tests initially failed by tripping the fallback guard on a single-fact deletion (pd.len()=1, total_derived=1), caught by adding temporary debug instrumentation to print the actual ratio rather than guessing from the assertion failure alone, then fixed by redesigning the padding to actually exercise the same rule under test (two-hop chains for transitivity; extra individuals of class A for equivalence) so the denominator is large enough for a genuine incremental-path test." .
